{"id":"urology-renal-tumors","order":8,"subjectKey":"urology","subject":"Urology","title":"Renal Tumors","subtitle":"Renal cell carcinoma, Wilms tumor and upper urinary tract urothelial carcinoma","sourceNote":"Derived only from the uploaded Renal tumors 5.pdf. Wording was normalized for revision without adding external guidelines or unsupported details.","subtopics":[{"id":"classification","label":"Classification of renal masses"},{"id":"rcc-epidemiology","label":"RCC epidemiology and risk factors"},{"id":"rcc-pathology","label":"RCC pathology and subtypes"},{"id":"rcc-clinical","label":"RCC clinical diagnosis and examination"},{"id":"rcc-investigations","label":"RCC investigations and imaging"},{"id":"rcc-treatment","label":"RCC treatment"},{"id":"wilms-pathology","label":"Wilms tumor pathology"},{"id":"wilms-clinical","label":"Wilms tumor clinical findings and differential"},{"id":"wilms-investigations","label":"Wilms tumor investigations"},{"id":"wilms-treatment","label":"Wilms tumor treatment and prognosis"},{"id":"utuc-epidemiology-risk","label":"Upper tract urothelial carcinoma epidemiology and risk"},{"id":"utuc-pathology-diagnosis","label":"Upper tract urothelial carcinoma pathology and diagnosis"},{"id":"utuc-treatment","label":"Upper tract urothelial carcinoma treatment"}],"cases":[{"id":"rt-c01","title":"Incidentally detected renal mass","topic":"RCC diagnosis","priority":"High","marks":8,"subtopics":["rcc-epidemiology","rcc-investigations","rcc-treatment"],"scenario":"A 66-year-old man with smoking, obesity and hypertension has an asymptomatic renal mass detected during abdominal imaging.","questions":["What is the likely malignant renal diagnosis?","List the risk factors stated in the lecture.","Which imaging study is used for diagnosis and staging?","State the broad surgical options according to tumor size."],"answer":["Renal cell carcinoma (RCC).","Smoking, obesity and hypertension; a first-degree relative and VHL disease also increase risk.","Contrast-enhanced CT is used for diagnosis and staging; MRI can diagnose and stage without ionizing radiation.","Partial nephrectomy for a small renal tumor; radical nephrectomy for a larger tumor."]},{"id":"rt-c02","title":"The classic RCC triad","topic":"RCC clinical diagnosis","priority":"High","marks":6,"subtopics":["rcc-clinical"],"scenario":"An adult presents with flank pain, painless gross hematuria and a palpable abdominal mass.","questions":["Name the triad.","What does its presence suggest?","How is RCC commonly discovered today?"],"answer":["The classic RCC triad: flank pain, painless gross hematuria and palpable abdominal mass.","The triad is rare and correlates with aggressive histology and advanced disease.","RCC is usually discovered incidentally."]},{"id":"rt-c03","title":"RCC with systemic manifestations","topic":"RCC paraneoplastic syndromes","priority":"High","marks":7,"subtopics":["rcc-clinical","rcc-investigations"],"scenario":"A patient with a renal mass has hypertension, hypercalcemia, polycythemia and abnormal liver function without hepatic metastasis.","questions":["What category of manifestations is described?","List the lecture examples.","Which laboratory tests are relevant?"],"answer":["Paraneoplastic syndromes of RCC.","Hypertension, hypercalcemia, polycythemia, non-metastatic hepatic dysfunction, fever and cachexia.","Urinalysis, CBC, creatinine and liver function tests."]},{"id":"rt-c04","title":"Renal mass with a non-reducible varicocele","topic":"RCC physical examination","priority":"Core","marks":5,"subtopics":["rcc-clinical"],"scenario":"An adult with a renal mass is found to have a non-reducible varicocele.","questions":["What mechanism is stated in the lecture?","Name two other examination findings."],"answer":["Obstruction of the testicular vein.","A flank or abdominal mass and a left supraclavicular lymph node (Virchow gland)."]},{"id":"rt-c05","title":"Four-year-old with an abdominal mass","topic":"Wilms tumor presentation","priority":"High","marks":8,"subtopics":["wilms-pathology","wilms-clinical"],"scenario":"A mother discovers a large abdominal mass in her four-year-old child. The child also has abdominal distention and hypertension.","questions":["What is the likely diagnosis?","From which tissue does it arise?","Why may hypertension occur?","List other clinical findings."],"answer":["Wilms tumor.","Embryonic nephrogenic tissue.","Hypertension may occur due to renin production.","Abdominal pain and distention, anorexia, nausea, vomiting and hematuria."]},{"id":"rt-c06","title":"Differential diagnosis of a childhood flank mass","topic":"Pediatric flank mass","priority":"Core","marks":5,"subtopics":["wilms-clinical"],"scenario":"A child is assessed for a palpable flank mass before imaging confirms the cause.","questions":["List the differential diagnoses stated in the lecture."],"answer":["Hydronephrosis.","Cystic kidney diseases.","Neuroblastoma.","Sarcomas."]},{"id":"rt-c07","title":"Imaging a suspected Wilms tumor","topic":"Wilms investigations","priority":"High","marks":6,"subtopics":["wilms-investigations"],"scenario":"A child with a suspected renal tumor needs laboratory and imaging assessment.","questions":["State the laboratory studies.","What is the primary imaging investigation?","Which investigation is preferred because it avoids ionizing radiation?"],"answer":["Urinalysis and CBC.","Ultrasound is the primary investigation of choice.","MRI is the investigation of choice because it has no ionizing radiation; abdominal CT is also used."]},{"id":"rt-c08","title":"Management of Wilms tumor","topic":"Wilms treatment","priority":"High","marks":6,"subtopics":["wilms-treatment"],"scenario":"A child is diagnosed with Wilms tumor.","questions":["List the treatment modalities stated in the lecture.","State the overall survival figure given."],"answer":["Nephrectomy, chemotherapy and radiotherapy.","Overall survival is up to 90%."]},{"id":"rt-c09","title":"Hematuria from an upper tract lesion","topic":"UTUC presentation","priority":"High","marks":8,"subtopics":["utuc-epidemiology-risk","utuc-pathology-diagnosis"],"scenario":"A male smoker presents with hematuria, dysuria and flank pain. CT urography suggests a lesion in the renal pelvis.","questions":["What is the likely diagnosis?","Where can this tumor arise?","State two epidemiologic facts from the lecture.","Which imaging study has the highest diagnostic accuracy?"],"answer":["Upper urinary tract urothelial carcinoma (UTUC).","The lining of the renal pelvis or ureter.","UTUC forms 5%-10% of urothelial tumors, is twice as frequent in men, is usually unilateral, and pelvicalyceal involvement is twice as common as ureteral involvement.","Computed tomography urography."]},{"id":"rt-c10","title":"Young patient with possible hereditary UTUC","topic":"Hereditary UTUC","priority":"Core","marks":6,"subtopics":["utuc-epidemiology-risk"],"scenario":"A patient younger than 60 years develops an upper tract urothelial tumor.","questions":["When should a hereditary tumor be considered?","Which syndrome is linked to hereditary UTUC?","State the tobacco-associated relative risk stated in the lecture."],"answer":["Familial or hereditary upper tract tumor should be considered when the patient is younger than 60 years.","Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal carcinoma (HNPCC), or Lynch syndrome.","Routine tobacco use increases the r